  "verbalization": "Penbutolol, Bupranolol, and Timolol share a common structural similarity with Gemfibrozil, which is known to treat Coronary Artery Disease. This relationship is supported by the evidence that Penbutolol and Bupranolol resemble Gemfibrozil, and that Gemfibrozil itself has a therapeutic effect on Coronary Artery Disease. The shared structural features between Penbutolol and Bupranolol, along with their resemblance to Gemfibrozil, suggest a potential mechanism of action involving similar pharmacological pathways. The lowest common ancestors of these entities—Beta-Adrenergic Antagonists and Heteroorganic Entities—highlight a broader class of compounds that may interact with biological targets in a manner that influences cardiovascular health. The evidence from multiple paths reinforces the idea that Penbutolol may have a therapeutic effect on Coronary Artery Disease through a mechanism that involves structural similarity to Gemfibrozil, potentially modulating lipid metabolism or other pathways associated with the disease.",
